=== task: cells medium
title: Trace the filter, item by item
prompt: For each value of `n`, what does this comprehension add to `result`? Type the number that gets added, or type `skip` if the `if` test is False for that item.
code:
```python
nums = [3, 8, 5, 10]
result = [n - 1 for n in nums if n > 4]
print(result)
```
labels: n = 3 | n = 8 | n = 5 | n = 10
answer: skip | 7 | 4 | 9
wrong[skip | 8 | 5 | 10]: The filter is right, but the comprehension adds the expression `n - 1`, not n itself: 8 - 1 = 7, 5 - 1 = 4, 10 - 1 = 9.
wrong[2 | 7 | 4 | 9]: 3 > 4 is False, so 3 is skipped completely. The expression `n - 1` is never worked out for it, and nothing is added.
wrong[skip | 7 | skip | 9]: The test checks the original item, not the new value. For n = 5 the test is `5 > 4`, which is True, so `5 - 1 = 4` is added. (Testing the new value 4 would give False, but that is not what the `if` tests.)
hint: For each item, do the `if` test first. Only if it is True, work out `n - 1`.
hint: The test uses the original item: is 3 > 4? Is 8 > 4? Is 5 > 4? Is 10 > 4?
hint: Only 3 fails the test. For each of the others, subtract 1.
right: Python takes each n, tests `n > 4`, and only for items that pass works out `n - 1`. 3 fails and is skipped; 8, 5 and 10 pass and give 7, 4 and 9. So `result` is `[7, 4, 9]`.
wrong: Go item by item, in loop order. First comes the test `n > 4` on the original item; if it is False, the item is skipped. If it is True, the expression `n - 1` decides what is added. 3 > 4 is False (skip). 8 > 4 is True (add 7). 5 > 4 is True (add 4). 10 > 4 is True (add 9).

=== task: parsons medium
title: Write the loop behind the comprehension
prompt: This comprehension keeps the words that are longer than 3 letters, in capitals:

